# Product Admin Dashboard

This project is a small admin dashboard built as part of a frontend assignment,where a logged-in user can view, search, filter, sort and manage a list of 
products using the free DummyJSON API. It has been developed using Next.js for the framework and routing, React for building the user interface, Tailwind CSS
for styling, and Axios for handling all communication with the API.

## Technologies Used

This project makes use of Next.js (App Router), React, Tailwind CSS and Axios. All data used in the application comes from the public DummyJSON API available 
at https://dummyjson.com, and every request made to this API goes through Axios rather than any other method.

## Setup Instructions

To run this project on your own computer, first make sure Node.js is installed. Then clone this repository, open a terminal inside the project folder, and run 
`npm install` to install all the required dependencies. Once the installation is complete, run `npm run dev` to start the development server, and open 
`http://localhost:3000` in your browser to view the application. To log in, use the username `emilys` and the password `emilyspass`, since these are the 
demo credentials provided by the DummyJSON API for authentication.

## Features Completed

In this project, I have implemented a complete login and logout flow where only authenticated users are able to access the product pages, and any user who is 
not logged in is automatically redirected to the login page. I have built a product listing page that displays each product's image, title, category, price, 
rating and stock, and this list is shown as a table on larger screens and as cards on smaller mobile screens. Pagination has been implemented so that products 
load page by page from the API, along with page number buttons, Previous and Next controls, a page size selector, and a text summary showing which products are 
currently being displayed out of the total. A debounced search feature has been added so that the application waits until the user stops typing before sending a 
request to the API, which also resets the results back to the first page. Category filtering and sorting by price, rating or title have also been included, along
with a detailed product page that shows images, a description and customer reviews, and displays a proper "not found" message if an invalid product id is entered. 
I have also implemented the ability to add, edit and delete products through a validated form and a confirmation popup before deletion, and I have added loading 
indicators, empty state messages and error messages with a retry option wherever data is being fetched from the API.

## Important Decisions and Approach

Since the DummyJSON API does not allow searching and filtering by category to be applied at the same time, I decided that whenever the user types something into 
the search box, the category filter will be temporarily disabled, and searching will take priority over filtering. I made this decision because it keeps the
behaviour of the application simple, predictable and easy for the user to understand. In addition, since the DummyJSON API does not actually persist any changes
made through add, edit or delete requests, I have stored these changes in the browser's local storage and merged them with the data received from the API, so that
the application still behaves as expected from the user's point of view even though the underlying API does not save the data permanently.

## A Problem I Faced and How I Solved It

One of the challenges I faced while building this project was that when a user typed quickly into the search box, older and slower API responses would 
sometimes arrive after newer ones and incorrectly overwrite the most recent results on the screen. I solved this problem by using the AbortController 
feature available in JavaScript, which allows a previous, still-pending request to be cancelled as soon as a new request is triggered, ensuring that only 
the response from the latest request is ever used to update the product list.

## Where AI Tools Were Used

I used AI tools during the development of this project to help me understand certain concepts more clearly, to review and refine the structure of my code, 
and to make sure I was following good practices throughout. However, I made sure to personally read through, understand and test every part of the code before 
including it in this project, and I am able to explain and modify any part of it if required.
